{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"5ebaef16-de6f-4b0d-baad-f3171019c71d","title":"Chance a dart lands in a circle inside a square"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"5ebaef16-de6f-4b0d-baad-f3171019c71d","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Assume the dart lands uniformly inside this square. The circle touches all four sides, so its diameter equals the square’s side length.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"target","region":"left","frame":{"x":[-1.5,1.5],"y":[-1.5,1.5],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"square","points":[[-1,-1],[1,-1],[1,1],[-1,1]],"closed":true},{"kind":"circle","id":"circle","center":[0,0],"r":1},{"kind":"measure","from":[-1,-1.25],"to":[1,-1.25],"label":"2r"}]},{"op":"text","id":"heading","region":"C1","content":"Chance = fraction of area"},{"op":"equation","id":"ratio","anchor":{"el":"heading","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"P(\\text{circle})=\\frac{A_{\\text{circle}}}{A_{\\text{square}}}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"5ebaef16-de6f-4b0d-baad-f3171019c71d","step":{"id":"s2","script":"The shaded circle contains all successful landing points. Its area is pi times radius squared; the square’s area is four times radius squared.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"success","on":"target","marks":[{"kind":"shade","inside":"circle"},{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[0,0]},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[1,0],"label":"r"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"areas","anchor":{"el":"ratio","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"A_{\\text{circle}}=\\pi r^2,\\qquad A_{\\text{square}}=(2r)^2=4r^2"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"5ebaef16-de6f-4b0d-baad-f3171019c71d","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Divide the areas: the radius cancels. The chance is about seventy-nine percent, regardless of size, provided the circle stays inscribed and landings are uniform.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"answer","anchor":{"el":"areas","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"P=\\frac{\\pi r^2}{4r^2}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\approx 0.785=78.5\\%"},{"op":"figure","id":"result","on":"target","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[0,0.5],"text":"78.5%","size":"large"}]}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If both the square and circle double in size, does the chance change?","expected_gist":"No. Both areas multiply by four, so their ratio remains pi divided by four."}}}
{"type":"lesson.done","request_id":"5ebaef16-de6f-4b0d-baad-f3171019c71d","accepted_steps":3,"repairs":0,"dropped_steps":0,"sanitized_steps":0,"sanitized_fields":0}
